 | `<Space>q`    | quit                            |
 | `<Space>Y`    | copy yank buffer / selection to the system clipboard |
 | `<Space>P`    | paste the system clipboard at the cursor |
+| `<Space>U`    | open the undo tree (branch list)  |
+
+`<Space>U` opens a popup listing every branch tip of the undo tree. The
+current branch is marked with `*`; `j`/`k` move, `Enter` jumps to the chosen
+branch (replaying its edits through the lowest common ancestor), `Esc` closes.
+Because the undo log is a tree, editing after an undo forks a new branch
+instead of discarding the redo history — nothing you type is ever lost.
+
+`u`/`Ctrl-R` undo and redo whole **operations**, not individual keystrokes: an
+operation is every edit done between breaks (cursor motion, `Esc`, saving or
+switching buffers). Typing a word, deleting a word with `xxxx`, or deleting a
+word and typing its replacement in one insert session are each a single
+`u`.
 
 `<Space>Y` copies whatever `y`/`yy`/visual-`y` last yanked, or the current
 visual selection, into the OS clipboard (via `pbcopy`/`pbpaste` on macOS,
@@ -143,7 +150,6 @@ extend the selection (`2j`, `10l`). `Esc` cancels a pending count or operator.
